'''Lake Hauroko''' is the [[List of lakes of New Zealand#Deepest lakes|deepest lake]] in New Zealand. The lake, which is {{convert|462|m}} deep,<ref name=":4">{{Cite web|last=Nathan|first=Simon|date=24 September 2007|title=New Zealand lakes|url=https://teara.govt.nz/en/lakes/page-1|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=6 July 2020|website=[[Te Ara: The Encyclopedia of New Zealand]] |language=en}}</ref> is located in a mountain valley in [[Fiordland National Park]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=December 2019|title=Fiordland Day Walks|url=https://www.doc.govt.nz/globalassets/documents/parks-and-recreation/tracks-and-walks/southland/fiordland-day-walks.pdf|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=6 July 2020|website=DOC}}</ref>

== Etymology ==
"Hauroko" translates from [[Māori language|te reo Māori]] as "[[wiktionary:soughing|soughing]] of the wind"<ref name=":3">{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=NZGB Gazetteer|url=https://gazetteer.linz.govt.nz/place/2670|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-07-06|website=gazetteer.linz.govt.nz}}</ref> or "sounding wind".<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=1968|title=Duff describing the discovery of the Lake Hauroko cave burial|url=https://teara.govt.nz/en/speech/180/duff-describing-the-discovery-of-the-lake-hauroko-cave-burial|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2020-07-06|website=[[Te Ara: The Encyclopedia of New Zealand]] |language=en}}</ref> Prior to 1930, the lake was also called "Lake Hauroto".<ref name=":3" />

== Geography ==
[[File:Wairaurahiri Jetboat parked on beach of Lake Hauroko.jpg|thumb|left|Jetboating on Lake Hauroko]]

Lake Hauroko is {{convert|462|m}} deep; sources range from calling it the 16th deepest lake in the world<ref name=":4" /> to the 23rd deepest.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Esler|first1=Lloyd|date=9 October 2014|title=Hauroko NZ's deepest lake|work=[[The Southland Times]]|url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/southland-times/culture/in-the-south/10541961/Hauroko-NZs-deepest-lake|accessdate=15 June 2015}}</ref> The S-shaped lake is {{convert|32|km|mi|abbr=}} long<ref name=":6" /> and has a surface area of {{convert|65|km2|sqmi|abbr=}}.<ref name=":6" /> The lake surface is about {{convert|155|m||abbr=}} above sea level.<ref name=":5" /> 

One of the country's southernmost lakes, it is only 13 km from the southern coast of the [[South Island]]. It sits between the similarly-sized lakes [[Lake Monowai|Monowai]] and [[Lake Poteriteri|Poteriteri]]. According to the 1925 New Zealand Official Yearbook, it drains about {{Convert|1800|ft3||abbr=on}}/sec<ref name=":6">{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=1925|title=Official Year Book 1925|url=https://www3.stats.govt.nz/New_Zealand_Official_Yearbooks/1925/NZOYB_1925.html|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=6 July 2020|website=Statistics NZ}}</ref> via the {{Convert|20|km||abbr=on}}-long [[Wairaurāhiri River]] into [[Foveaux Strait]] 10 kilometres to the west of [[Te Waewae Bay]].

The largest island in Lake Hauroko is Mary Island, named in 1883 after the wife of the [[Surveyor General|government surveyor]] John Hay.<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=NEWSLETTER HIGHLIGHTS - John Hay|url=http://www.balcluthagenealogy.org.nz/04_2005_2.htm|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2018-03-30|website=www.balcluthagenealogy.org.nz}}</ref>{{Better source needed|date=July 2020|reason=Current source is probably not a reliable one.}} A smaller island is adjacent to Teal Bay at the southern end of the lake. There are also a few other smaller islets and rocks in the lake.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web|title=New Zealand Topographic Map|url=https://www.topomap.co.nz/NZTopoMap?v=2&ll=-46.012283,167.307873&z=13|access-date=2020-07-06|website=NZ Topo Map|language=en}}</ref>

== Geology ==
Traditionally, Ngā Puna Wai Karikari o Rakaihautu say the lake was dug by [[rangatira]] [[Rākaihautū]] on his journey south with his [[Foot plough#New Zealand|ko]]. The lake is a Statutory Acknowledgement site under the [[Ngāi Tahu#Governance|Ngāi Tahu Claims Settlement Act 1998]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.es.govt.nz/Document%20Library/Plans,%20policies%20and%20strategies/Regional%20plans/Iwi%20Management%20Plan/Te%20Tangi%20a%20Tauira%20-%20The%20Cry%20of%20the%20People.pdf|title=Ngāi Tahu ki Murihiku Natural Resource and Environmental Iwi Management Plan|last=|first=|date=2008|website=|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=}}</ref>

From a geological perspective, it is a [[glacial lake]]<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|date=2007-02-28|title=Freshwater feature: Lake Hauroko, Fiordland|language=en|work=NIWA|url=https://www.niwa.co.nz/freshwater-and-estuaries/freshwater-and-estuaries-update/no02-2003/freshwater-feature-lake-hauroko-fiordland|access-date=2018-03-31}}</ref> formed near the Hauroko [[Fault (geology)|Fault]]. It and other faults in the area have slipped several kilometres.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://theses.otagogeology.org.nz/items/show/210|title=Startigraphy and provenance of Tertiary sediments at Lake Hauroko, with reference to dextral strike-slip movementon the Moonlight Fault system. · Otago Geology Theses|website=theses.otagogeology.org.nz|language=en-US|access-date=2018-03-31}}</ref> The higher ground consists of early [[carboniferous]] granites and [[Metamorphic rock|metamorphic]] rocks, with much more recent Hauroko Formation [[Eocene]] - [[Oligocene]] sediments such as [[calcareous]] [[sandstone]], on the lower ground, towards the southeast.<ref name=":2">{{Cite journal|title=Plutonic rocks of the Median Batholith in eastern and central Fiordland, New Zealand: Field relations, geochemistry, correlation, and nomenclature|journal=New Zealand Journal of Geology and Geophysics|volume=52|issue=2|pages=101–148|date=2009|doi=10.1080/00288300909509882|last1 = Allibone|first1 = A. H.|last2=Jongens|first2=R.|last3=Scott|first3=J. M.|last4=Tulloch|first4=A. J.|last5=Turnbull|first5=I. M.|last6=Cooper|first6=A. F.|last7=Powell|first7=N. G.|last8=Ladley|first8=E. B.|last9=King|first9=R. P.|last10=Rattenbury|first10=M. S.|s2cid=129838388 }}</ref>

Hauroko granite is about 358 million years old, medium grained, white, [[granodiorite]] and [[granite]], with red-brown [[biotite]]. It has [[Intrusive rock|intruded]] [[Dike swarm|dikes]], [[Volcanic plug|plugs]] and [[xenolith]]s into [[Metasedimentary rock|metasediment]]. Albert Edward Granite is coarser, with pink [[Orthoclase|K-feldspar]] [[megacryst]]s and green-brown biotite. There are also small amounts of carboniferous [[diorite]].<ref name=":2" /> Towards the northwest of the lake, the area between the Hauroko Burn and the Hay River has dioritic, [[felsic]] dykes.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://theses.otagogeology.org.nz/items/show/117|title=Geology of an area between the Hauroko Burn and the Hay River, southern Fiordland. · Otago Geology Theses|website=theses.otagogeology.org.nz|language=en-US|access-date=2018-03-31}}</ref>

== Access ==
A {{Convert|30|km||abbr=on}} road from [[Clifden, New Zealand|Clifden]]<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=Lake Hauroko Lookout Track|url=http://www.doc.govt.nz/parks-and-recreation/places-to-go/fiordland/places/fiordland-national-park/things-to-do/tracks/lake-hauroko-lookout-track/|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2018-03-31|website=[[Department of Conservation (New Zealand)|Department of Conservation]]|language=en}}</ref> to the lake was built in about 1909<ref>{{Cite news|date=1909-05-03|title=A Visit to Lake Hauroko|pages=5|work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |url=https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/newspapers/ODT19090503.2.14|access-date=2018-03-31}}</ref> and improved in the 1960s.<ref name=":0" /> A water taxi runs to the [[Dusky Sound#Access|Dusky Track]], which starts at the north end of the lake and leads to [[Lake Manapouri]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=Dusky Track Transport|url=https://tripsandtramps.com/product-detail/dusky-track-transport|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2018-03-31|website=tripsandtramps.com|language=en}}</ref>

== Burial on Mary Island ==
Mary Island is the subject of several local myths, including one that the island is subject to a Māori curse. Such stories are dismissed by local Māori. The island is famous for the discovery of a burial site of a Māori woman in 1967, who is known as "the lady of the lake" by Southland locals. The burial site is in a cave on the eastern side of the island. Believed to have been placed on the burial site sometime between the late 16th century and 17th century, possibly around 1660, the woman was laid to rest wearing a flax cloak and a dog skin collar with weka feather edging around her neck, and was seated upright on a bier made of sticks and leaves. The reasons for the burial in this manner are uncertain, although it has been suggested that these burials were to either make sure the remains were protected from desecration by enemies, or to protect living descendants from a dangerous "[[Tapu (Polynesian culture)|tapu]]" (sacred, forbidden or taboo<ref>{{cite web|last1=Moorefield|first1=John C|title=Tapu|url=http://www.maoridictionary.co.nz/word/7504|website=Maori Dictionary - Te Aka Māori-English, English-Māori Dictionary|accessdate=6 September 2015}}</ref>) that Māori may have believed the ancestral bones possessed. This led to the belief that this woman was of high-ranking status, and it was later discovered through an archaeological investigation that she was a chieftain of the Ngāti Mamoe tribe. The burial remains on the island today, with a grille made of steel and wire mesh ensuring that people can still view the burial, but the woman will remain untouched.<ref name="Duff, Roger Shepherd">{{cite web|last1=Davidson|first1=Janet|authorlink1=Dictionary of New Zealand Biography|title=Duff, Roger Shepherd|url=http://www.TeAra.govt.nz/en/biographies/5d27/duff-roger-shepherd|accessdate=6 September 2015|website=[[Te Ara - the Encyclopedia of New Zealand]] |publisher=The New Zealand Government}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|last1=Simmons|first1=D. R.|title=The Lake Hauroko Burial: Preliminary Report|journal=New Zealand Archaeological Association Newsletter|date=20 April 1967|pages=66–68}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|author=New Zealand Historic Places Trust Pouhere Taonga|title=Kōiwi Tangata Human Remains|journal=Archaeological Guidelines Series|date=22 February 2010|page=12}}</ref>

== Natural history ==
Trees are mainly [[Prumnopitys taxifolia|mataī]], [[Podocarpus totara|tōtara]], [[Dacrydium cupressinum|rimu]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.murrells.co.nz/files/Fiordland-Day-Walks.pdf|title=Fiordland Day Walks|last=|first=|date=|website=|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=}}</ref> [[Fuscospora solandri|tawhai pango]], [[Lophozonia menziesii|tawhai]], [[Pseudopanax|''Pseudopanax linearis'']] and rautawhiri (''[[Pittosporum]] Colensoi'').<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://rsnz.natlib.govt.nz/volume/rsnz_43/rsnz_43_00_004440.html|title=Transactions of the New Zealand Institute, 1910. Notes on the Botany of Lake Hauroko District.|last=|first=|date=|website=rsnz.natlib.govt.nz|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=2018-03-31}}</ref>

The lake is one of the few not yet colonised by invasive water weeds, except the bulbous rush, ''[[Juncus|Juncus bulbosus]],<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal|title=Submerged vegetation of Lakes Te Anau, Manapouri, Monowai, Hauroko, and Poteriteri|journal = New Zealand Journal of Marine and Freshwater Research|volume = 32|issue = 4|pages = 621–638|last=NIWA|date=1998|doi=10.1080/00288330.1998.9516849|doi-access=free}}</ref>'' so it retains plants such as ''[[Nitella|Nitella stuartii]]'' algae, ''[[Callitriche|Callitriche petriei]]'' water-starwort, ''[[Isoetes|Isoetes kirkii]]'' quillwort, ''[[Pilularia|Pilularia novaezelandiae]]'' pillwort fern'','' ''[[Trithuria inconspicua]]'' waterlilies<ref name=":0" /> and ''[[Stonewort|Charales fibrosa]].''<ref name=":1" />

Pests include [[Stoats in New Zealand|stoats]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.biosecurity.org.nz/site/assets/files/1191/protect-winter-2010.pdf|title=Using genetics to confirm stoat reinvasion of Fiordland islands|last=Edge|first=Kerri-Anne|date=2010|website=New Zealand Biosecurity Institute|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=}}</ref> and [[Common brushtail possum in New Zealand|possums]], which arrived in the 1990s.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.doc.govt.nz/Documents/science-and-technical/NZmistletoe2.pdf|title=Threats to New Zealand mistletoes|last=|first=|date=1997|website=DoC|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=}}</ref> They are controlled by trapping, bait stations<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.fishing.net.nz/forum/1080-and-environment-waikato_topic85639.html|title=1080 and Environment Waikato|work=The Fishing Website : Discussion Forums|access-date=2018-03-31|language=en}}</ref> and periodic [[1080 usage in New Zealand|1080]] drops.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/southland-times/news/10422661/Farmer-gets-police-escort-from-drop-zone|title=Farmer gets police escort from drop zone|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=25 August 2014 |language=en|access-date=2018-03-31}}</ref>

